ARRAYS

Arrays help us define a list of values according to a set data type, eliminating the need to define individual variables and wasting time and memory.

Its usual syntax looks like this:

data-type[the range of the array, in this case a singular numerical digit] arrayname = {whatever you want in here};

EX:
  int[5] myCuteNumberList = {44, 92, 1123, 4432, 33};

Every position in an array is called an INDEX, said index numbers range from 0 to the size of your array-1, EX: this array ranges 0,1,2,3,4 for all 5 digits of its size.
The index number is helpful for modifying a specific part of an array,

Using the example from earlier:

If we wanted to modify the number '1123' from our array, we'd only have to do the following

myCuteNumberList[2] = 77;

To automatically get the length of an array whose length isint already specified, you can append .Length to your array's name and print it out or use it wherever that value
may be of use to you.

